  "query_preview": "..\n\nNext 7 days (Through Saturday)...\n\nShort wave ridging today ahead of an approaching upper low over \nthe Pacific NW will lead to warmer and mainly dry conditions \nacross our CWA today. Highs will range from lower 70s to lower \n80s. The pattern will also induce brisk southeast winds across our\neastern border counties with gusts 25 to 40 mph by the afternoon.\nModels track the Pacific low eastward into the inter mountain \nRockies by evening with mid level flow backing strongly to the \nsouthwest over our region. \n\nAs the low tracks across the region Sunday night into Monday, and\nassociated cold front pushes through our area, we should see \nisolated to scattered showers and a few thunderstorms develop. \nHowever, the best upper dynamics are progged to track north of our\nCWA limiting potential rainfall. That said, there is a brief \nwindow of decent CAPE and shear in our southeast corner (Carter \nCounty) where CAMs are suggesting some convection. Instability is \nlimited, but we could see a strong cell or two in that area toward\nmid to late evening. Snow showers are possible over the highest \nelevations, though little in the way of accumulation is expected. \n\nBehind the front, Monday will feature dry, cooler and breezy \nconditions. Highs will be range from 60s west to around 70 east. \nWidespread westerly wind gusts of 30 to 50 mph can be expected, \nwith the greatest wind gusts along the western foothills. \nLocations from Big Timber north to Judith Gap have roughly a \n40-70% chance at seeing wind gusts of 50 mph or greater. Models \nseems to be flip flopping on the strength of 700 mb winds, so have\nheld off on any wind highlites at this time, but will continue to\nmonitor the US-191 corridor (Big Timber to Judith Gap) for \nMonday. As winds drop and skies clear out Monday night, frost is \npossible (20-40% chance) for some of the colder valley locations.\n\nEnsembles still signal NW to cyclonic flow behind the departing \nupper low will keep temps a little cooler than normal through \nmidweek. Occasional shortwaves may bring a chance of light showers\nto the area at times, most notably in the Thursday/Friday time \nframe according to the ECMWF. However, ensembles are not in \nagreement with this, so a good deal of uncertainty for now. Highs \nare forecast to range from 60s to lower 70s through Wednesday. \nTemperatures in the upper 60s to 70s are progged for Thursday and \nFriday. We may see lows in the 30s for Monday night/Tuesday \nmorning with a slight chance of frost possible in the south \ncentral MT foothills. Otherwise, lows will be mainly 40s to lower\n50s this week. BT",
